component SigilImplementationOwnership {
  goal {
    Provide the shared core query that returns implementation targets linked to a
    Sigil contract from implementation comments.

    Keep implementation-to-contract ownership discovery deterministic and reusable
    for CLI and LSP without writing annotations into Sigil source.
  }

  interface {
    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      ownedImplementationTargetsFor(resolvedWorkspace, implementationSources,
      componentName, conceptName?, sectionName?) returns an owned-implementation
      projection or undefined

      The query scans implementation comments that link entrypoint definitions or
      agent-facing Markdown back to the selected Sigil component and optional concept
      and section scopes.
    }

    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      includes the owning contract, optional concept and section scopes, normalized
      implementation targets, and diagnostics
    }

    OwnedImplementationTarget {
      each target identifies one owned implementation artifact

      each target preserves the artifact kind, file path, selected sections, and
      optional symbol identity when available
    }

    ImplementationSourceSupport {
      isSupportedImplementationSource(filePath) returns whether the path can contain
      a supported ownership annotation

      supportedImplementationSourceGlobPatterns returns immutable workspace watcher
      patterns covering the same supported source extensions
    }

    ImplementationAnnotation {
      identifies a relation, repository-relative Sigil path, component name, optional
      concept name, and one or more selected sections

      the containing implementation file and adjacent entrypoint definition identify
      the owned implementation target

      uses @sigil <relation> <file>::<component>[::<concept>]
      <section>[,<section>...]

      file is a repository-relative Sigil path

      implements links an artifact that implements the selected section occurrences.

      uses links an artifact that uses the selected section occurrences.

      tests links an artifact that tests the selected section occurrences.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilImplementationOwnership {
  logic {
    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      scan supplied implementation sources for supported ownership comments

      resolve each annotation target against the selected Sigil workspace

      resolve each annotation's selected sections against the targeted component or
      concept occurrences

      normalize code, tests, and agent-facing workflow Markdown into one shared
      result shape

      derive symbol-level targets from annotated language entrypoint definitions and
      use file-level targets for Markdown
    }
  }

  constraints {
    AnnotationStorage {
      Ownership annotations exist only in implementation comments and never in Sigil
      source.

      Each annotation points from an implementation artifact to a repository-relative
      Sigil path, component, optional concept, and selected sections.
    }

    SectionSelection {
      Every annotation selects one or more interface, state, logic, constraints, or
      cases sections.

      Goal and decisions sections cannot be ownership targets.

      For a component target, each selector resolves to an existing section
      occurrence on the component or one of its matching expands.

      For a concept target, each selector resolves to an occurrence of that concept
      in the selected section.

      Empty, repeated, unknown, or unresolved section selectors produce diagnostics
      instead of an inferred relationship.
    }

    AnnotationPlacement {
      A source annotation is immediately associated with a language entrypoint
      definition such as a class, function, method, interface, struct, or equivalent
      stable definition.

      One annotation uses the language's single-line comment form.

      Multiple annotations use the language's multiline comment form.

      Markdown annotations use HTML comments.

      JSON annotations are outside the current scope.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      v1 includes source code and tests with supported comment syntax.

      v1 includes Markdown only for agent-facing instructions or workflow documents.

      Sigil sources, JSON, general prose documentation, configs, and non-text
      artifacts remain out of scope.

      The exported support predicate and watcher patterns describe one shared
      implementation-source extension policy.

      Every watcher-pattern match is accepted by isSupportedImplementationSource,
      while the predicate remains authoritative for received paths.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Resolve source annotations to their adjacent entrypoint definition.

      Resolve agent-facing Markdown annotations at file level.

      Ignore ambiguous or detached annotations and return diagnostics rather than
      inferring ownership.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    AnnotationStorage {
      Decision: Store ownership links in implementation comments that point back to
      governing Sigil components and concepts.

      Scope: Governs shared CLI and LSP ownership discovery for source code, tests,
      and agent-facing Markdown; excludes annotations inside Sigil and JSON support.

      Trade-offs: Implementation-local links are visible where code changes occur,
      but scanners must understand language comment and entrypoint syntax.

      Design issues addressed: Sigil contracts should remain
      implementation-independent while code retains a reviewable link to its
      governing rationale.

      Consequences: Ownership discovery scans implementation artifacts, and Sigil
      files contain no implementation-link annotations.

      Revisit when: A language cannot expose a stable comment or entrypoint
      association.
    }

    AnnotationGrammar {
      Decision: Encode each ownership link as @sigil <relation>
      <file>::<component>[::<concept>] <section>[,<section>...] and allow only
      implements, uses, or tests.

      Scope: Governs payloads parsed from supported implementation comments; file is
      a repository-relative Sigil path, component is required, concept is optional,
      and one or more sections are required.

      Trade-offs: A small fixed vocabulary and required occurrence selectors keep
      ownership queries precise and deterministic, but existing sectionless
      annotations require reviewed migration.

      Design issues addressed: Component-level and concept-level links otherwise
      cannot identify which contract occurrences the implementation uses, implements,
      or tests.

      Discarded alternatives: follows, validates, related, goal and decisions
      selectors, and sectionless annotations do not provide the selected
      implementation-occurrence precision required by the ownership query.

      Consequences: Parsers reject unsupported relations and annotations without
      valid implementation-related section selectors.

      Revisit when: Ownership queries need another behavioral relation or section
      occurrences are insufficiently precise.
    }

    AnnotationPlacement {
      Decision: Attach annotations to stable language entrypoint definitions, using
      single-line comments for one link and multiline comments for multiple links.

      Scope: Governs source annotation placement and comment form; Markdown uses HTML
      comments and remains file-scoped.

      Trade-offs: Entrypoint placement improves symbol navigation but requires
      language-aware scanning.

      Consequences: Classes, functions, methods, interfaces, structs, and equivalent
      definitions become linkable ownership entrypoints.

      Revisit when: Supported languages require declaration metadata instead of
      comments.
    }

    ScopeSelection {
      Decision: Allow optional concept and section scopes so a contract can return
      all owned targets, one concept's targets, or only targets selecting one section
      occurrence.

      Scope: Governs query scoping within one contract; section scope filters
      selected annotation sections and excludes cross-contract aggregation and
      transitive ownership inference.

      Trade-offs: Concept and section scoping increase precision without forcing
      separate query shapes, but callers must supply the occurrence scope when they
      need a narrower view.

      Consequences: The same API serves whole-contract, concept-specific, and
      section-specific ownership views.

      Revisit when: Component, concept, and section scoping prove too coarse or too
      fine for routine use.
    }

    SectionSelection {
      Decision: Resolve component-target selectors against component and
      matching-expand sections, and resolve concept-target selectors only against
      sections containing that concept.

      Scope: Governs annotation validation, projection metadata, and optional
      section-scoped lookup; excludes goal, decisions, and semantic-unit-level
      selectors.

      Trade-offs: Occurrence validation detects stale or impossible links, but moving
      a concept between sections requires its annotations to be reconciled.

      Design issues addressed: A section name has different precision when the
      annotation targets an entire component versus one concept.

      Consequences: Component annotations may select implementation-related component
      or expand sections, while concept annotations may select only
      implementation-related sections where that concept occurs.

      Revisit when: Ownership needs semantic-unit-level or cross-component occurrence
      selection.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      Decision: Limit v1 implementation targets to source code and tests with
      supported comments plus agent-facing instruction or workflow Markdown, and
      expose one shared source-support policy for host filtering and watcher
      registration.

      Scope: Governs eligible implementation artifacts, the support predicate, and
      extension-based watcher patterns; excludes filesystem traversal and
      host-specific watcher lifecycle.

      Trade-offs: Shared patterns prevent host integrations from duplicating the
      extension list, while received paths still require predicate validation.

      Consequences: CLI, LSP, and editor integrations can share one definition of
      supported ownership sources.

      Revisit when: Other artifact classes become meaningful implementation evidence
      or a supported host cannot express the shared patterns.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Decision: Resolve source annotations to adjacent language entrypoint
      definitions and resolve agent-facing Markdown at file level.

      Scope: Governs how owned implementation targets are normalized inside the query
      result; excludes detached-annotation inference and human approval.

      Trade-offs: Language-aware entrypoint resolution improves navigation, while
      Markdown remains useful through file-level links.

      Consequences: The same query can link source and test entrypoints plus
      file-level Markdown targets without guessing ambiguous ownership.

      Revisit when: Supported languages need declaration metadata or Markdown
      requires section-level targets.
    }
  }

  cases {
    ImplementationSourceSupport {
      A supported source path is accepted by the predicate and matched by a shared
      watcher pattern.

      Sigil, JSON, unsupported, and extensionless paths are rejected by the
      predicate.
    }

    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      A TypeScript line comment immediately before one function links that function
      to a Sigil component or concept.

      A language multiline comment contains multiple ownership annotations for one
      class, method, interface, struct, or equivalent entrypoint.

      An HTML comment links an agent-facing Markdown workflow file to a Sigil
      component or concept.

      A component annotation can implement logic and constraints occurrences without
      naming a concept.

      A concept annotation can use the interface occurrence of that concept.

      JSON content is ignored.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      A source annotation adjacent to a supported entrypoint produces a symbol-level
      implementation target.

      A Markdown instruction comment produces a file-level implementation target.

      A detached or ambiguous annotation produces a diagnostic instead of an inferred
      target.

      A concept annotation naming a section where the concept does not occur produces
      a diagnostic.
    }
  }
}
